  "query_preview": "..\n\nCalm weather today with some high clouds streaming into the \narea this afternoon due to a shift in upper level flow to be \nsouthwesterly. This shift in the upper level flow is preceding\na deepening Pacific trough that will push into the region \nstarting tomorrow. This trough brings along a subtropical jet \nthat will carry with it anomalously high moisture leading to a \nslight chance for showers and thunderstorms tomorrow afternoon. \nThese showers and thunderstorms will be focused to the AZ/NM \nborder with a periphery as far west as central Graham and \ncentral Cochise counties. Moisture with PWAT values upwards of 1\ninch will broaden into the region Friday and combined with the \nlocation of the subtropical jet, expand the chances for showers \nand thunderstorms. Friday, showers and thunderstorms will be \nfocused to northern Pima through Pinal, Graham, and Greenlee \ncounties. In these areas there is a potential for greater \nrainfall amounts as repeated rounds or training thunderstorms \nmay produce a flash flood threat. Flooding potential will depend\non how well instability can develop during the day, which will \nalso impact a low end but non-zero severe thunderstorm threat. \nHeavy rainfall rates, isolated strong gusty winds, and small \nhail may accompany any thunderstorm Friday. Rainfall totals \nacross the area Friday range between 0.1 to 0.25 inches in the \nvalleys and 0.25 to 0.4 inches in the mountains. \n\nThe aforementioned upper level trough that will bring unsettled\nweather to the area Friday will move out of the area fairly \nquickly as a stronger Pacific trough moves into the area\nSaturday night. Saturday during the day, lingering moisture \nwill remain in the area however chances for isolated showers and\nthunderstorms is minimal. \n\nEarly Sunday into early Monday a more substantial Pacific \ntrough will impact the region with a strong associated jet \nstreak. Significant moisture will be transported to the region \nwith PWATs up to 1.5 inches possible. In NAEFS climatology, \nprecipitable water and integrated water vapor transport values \nrange from the 99th percentile to the max of climatology, \nhighlighting the potential significance of this event. This \nmoisture combined with synoptic forcing will lead to widespread \nrain with isolated embedded thunderstorms across the region. \nRainfall throughout the period will be longer duration with \nperiods of moderate to locally heavy rainfall rates. Rainfall \ntotals for Sunday into Monday range from 0.5 to 1 inch in the \nvalleys with 1 to 2+ inches in the mountains and in portions of \nPinal, Graham, and Greenlee counties. High rainfall amounts will\nbe focused to the Catalinas/Rincons northeast through the White\nMountains. Flash flooding potential returns Sunday with more \nsubstantial flows that may develop in creeks and rivers of \nnorthern Graham and Greenlee counties, including the San \nFrancisco, Blue, and Gila rivers. Depending on the magnitude and\nplacement of the heaviest rainfall, impacts within these \nwatersheds could continue into early next week.",
